WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.281
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.581 --> 00:00:09.455
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.755 --> 00:00:16.459
Unit twenty-two: Research Ethics. Today you will express formal obligations with 'must' and 'be required to'.

4
00:00:16.759 --> 00:00:19.469
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.620
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.438
Ethics.

7
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.378
Ethics means moral principles governing conduct.

8
00:00:29.983 --> 00:00:31.971
The ethics committee meets monthly.

9
00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:35.294
Your turn. Say: Ethics.

10
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:39.227
Ethics.

11
00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:41.333
Informed consent.

12
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:47.010
Informed consent means voluntary agreement given with full understanding of risks.

13
00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:50.510
Informed consent must be documented in writing.

14
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.908
Your turn. Say: Informed consent.

15
00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:58.383
Informed consent.

16
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.536
Confidentiality.

17
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.732
Confidentiality means the protection of personal information from disclosure.

18
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:09.582
Confidentiality was guaranteed to all participants.

19
00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:13.648
Your turn. Say: Confidentiality.

20
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.919
Confidentiality.

21
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:19.835
Participant.

22
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.941
Participant means a person who takes part in a study.

23
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.130
Participants may withdraw at any time.

24
00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:30.404
Your turn. Say: Participant.

25
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:34.268
Participant.

26
00:01:35.017 --> 00:01:35.797
Harm.

27
00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:40.083
Harm means physical, psychological or social damage.

28
00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:43.379
Researchers must minimise any potential harm.

29
00:01:43.983 --> 00:01:46.427
Your turn. Say: Harm.

30
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:50.175
Harm.

31
00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:52.011
Anonymity.

32
00:01:53.017 --> 00:01:56.618
Anonymity means the removal of all identifying information.

33
00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:59.866
Anonymity is stronger than confidentiality.

34
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.195
Your turn. Say: Anonymity.

35
00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:06.953
Anonymity.

36
00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:08.845
Approval.

37
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:13.080
Approval means formal permission granted by an ethics board.

38
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:16.379
The study cannot begin without ethical approval.

39
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:19.427
Your turn. Say: Approval.

40
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:23.279
Approval.

41
00:02:24.017 --> 00:02:25.064
Vulnerable.

42
00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:29.466
Vulnerable means at greater risk of harm or exploitation.

43
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.444
Children are considered a vulnerable group.

44
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.400
Your turn. Say: Vulnerable.

45
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:39.314
Vulnerable.

46
00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.820
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.683 --> 00:02:49.681
Visal, your project involves interviewing secondary students. What must you do first?

48
00:02:50.783 --> 00:03:00.390
Researchers are required to obtain informed consent. Because the participants are under eighteen, parents must also sign.

49
00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:04.354
What if a student changes their mind halfway through?

50
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:12.140
They must be allowed to withdraw at any time, without giving a reason and without any penalty.

51
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.586
And how will you protect their identities?

52
00:03:16.683 --> 00:03:24.801
All names are required to be removed from transcripts, and recordings must be deleted once the analysis is complete.

53
00:03:25.967 --> 00:03:27.270
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:32.067
Grammar focus: Obligation with must and be required to.

55
00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:33.530
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.217 --> 00:03:37.342
Researchers are required to obtain informed consent.

57
00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.329
Participants must be allowed to withdraw at any time.

58
00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:45.447
All names are required to be removed from transcripts.

59
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:58.180
'Must' expresses strong obligation, often from the speaker or a rule. 'Be required to' is more formal and emphasises an external regulation. Both can take a passive infinitive: 'must be allowed'.

60
00:03:58.917 --> 00:04:03.531
Be careful! Don't say: Researchers are required obtaining consent.

61
00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:08.465
Say: Researchers are required to obtain consent.

62
00:04:08.765 --> 00:04:11.136
Use 'be required to' + base verb.

63
00:04:11.933 --> 00:04:14.690
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.337
Protecting Participants.

65
00:04:17.637 --> 00:04:24.269
Research ethics developed largely in response to historical abuses in which participants were harmed without their knowledge.

66
00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:30.262
Today, researchers are required to obtain informed consent before any data is collected.

67
00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:36.784
Participants must understand the risks involved, and they must be free to withdraw at any stage.

68
00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.654
Additional safeguards are required when working with vulnerable groups, such as children or refugees.

69
00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:49.615
No study involving people may begin until it has received formal ethical approval.

70
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.551
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:55.417 --> 00:04:58.391
Researchers are required blank informed consent.

72
00:04:58.691 --> 00:05:02.229
A, obtaining. B, to obtain. C, obtain.

73
00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:07.894
The answer is B: to obtain.

74
00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.827
Participants must blank to withdraw at any time.

75
00:05:12.127 --> 00:05:15.560
A, allow. B, be allowed. C, allowed.

76
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.158
The answer is B: be allowed.

77
00:05:22.317 --> 00:05:25.338
Removing all identifying information ensures blank.

78
00:05:25.638 --> 00:05:29.454
A, anonymity. B, approval. C, harm.

79
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.965
The answer is A: anonymity.

80
00:05:36.117 --> 00:05:38.534
Which is the most formal expression of a rule?

81
00:05:38.834 --> 00:05:44.937
A, You've got to ask them.. B, Researchers are required to ask them.. C, You should probably ask them..

82
00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:52.044
The answer is B: Researchers are required to ask them..

83
00:05:53.183 --> 00:05:56.367
According to the reading, why did research ethics develop?

84
00:05:56.667 --> 00:06:02.840
A, in response to historical abuses. B, to speed up research. C, to reduce funding.

85
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.187
The answer is A: in response to historical abuses.

86
00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.319
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:15.183 --> 00:06:18.309
Why is informed consent essential in research with people?

88
00:06:35.117 --> 00:06:36.408
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:36.708 --> 00:07:08.219
Informed consent is essential because it respects people's right to decide what happens to them. Participants must understand the purpose, the risks and their right to withdraw before they agree. History shows what can happen without it: in some infamous studies, people were seriously harmed without ever being told the truth. Some researchers argue that full disclosure can bias results. Even so, I believe no scientific goal justifies treating people as mere data.

90
00:07:08.519 --> 00:07:10.193
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:11.083 --> 00:07:16.000
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and obligation with must and be required to.

92
00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.672
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

93
00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:23.530
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.470
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

